WEBVTT

NOTE Narration of How BioTSR works, as spoken.

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.340
How BioTSR works.

2
00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:07.600
Ubiquitin: one carbon alpha atom per residue.

3
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.100
Pick three from its hydrophobic patch.

4
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.970
Three points, one triangle, one number.

5
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.720
Each residue type has a fixed code.

6
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.910
Its shape gives two bins: angle and distance.

7
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.640
Codes and bins make one key.

8
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:32.630
The key records only residue types and shape bins, so different triangles can share it.

9
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:40.950
Any three carbon alpha atoms form a triangle: 70,300 in all.

10
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.690
Together, they produce 45,349 distinct keys.

11
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.110
Next, barnase, bound to its inhibitor, barstar.

12
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.800
The carbon alpha cross method keeps only residues at the interface.

13
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.360
Every cross triangle spans both proteins, like this one.

14
00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:08.790
Now compare three samples, each with its own keys.

15
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:15.360
9,523 distinct keys occur in all three samples.

16
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.720
BioTSR is open source, from the TSR Research Group.
